{
  "name": "Starfield",
  "created": "2026-09-13T18:00:00.000Z",
  "notes": "A night sky: stars that fade in, hold and fade out where they are, over a faint deep-blue sky, with a shooting star every so often.\n\nThe stars are `particles` with no speed, no gravity and `fade` 0, because fade only fades OUT and a star has to come up as well. The twinkle lives in the colour instead, as in `37-foxfire`: coloured by AGE through a palette that starts and ends black, so each star's whole life is one rise and fall. The palette is left unboosted on purpose -- saturation 2 would turn the near-white entries into pure hues, and stars are white. A perlin LFO on the birth rate clusters them; a slower perlin varies that one's rate.\n\nThe shooting star is `downpour`'s lightning chain with a shape where the flash was: a `sample_hold` draw has to clear 0.85 to trip the threshold, about one draw in thirteen at a quarter-hertz, so a streak arrives every minute or so and never on a schedule. The same draw sets where it starts. Its tail is a `fade` warp listed ABOVE the colour row, so the palette colours the tail as it decays -- white at the head, blue behind.",
